 The vector at t=3 can be found by solving a system of linear equations. Let the vector on the line at $t = 0$ be $\mathbf{v}$, and the direction vector of the line be $\mathbf{d}$. The vector at $t = -1$ is $\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{d} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix}$, and the vector at $t = 2$ is $\mathbf{v} + 2\mathbf{d} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}$. We can solve for $\mathbf{v}$ and $\mathbf{d}$ by solving the system: \[
\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{d} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix}
\] \[
\mathbf{v} + 2\mathbf{d} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}
\] Subtract the first equation from the second to eliminate $\mathbf{v}$ and solve for $\mathbf{d}$. The resulting system is: \[
\mathbf{d} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}
\] \[
\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 8 \\ 20 \end{pmatrix}
\] The vector at $t = 3$ is then $\mathbf{v} + 3\mathbf{d} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 8 \\ 20 \end{pmatrix} + 3 \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 8 - 15 \\ 20 - 36 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ -16 \end{pmatrix} \] </think> <answer> $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ -16 \end{pmatrix}$ </answer>

